<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Thames</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Thames is the longest river wholly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, flowing about 346 km from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotswolds">Cotswolds</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. Since prehistoric times it has been a highway and a boundary, separating Celtic peoples and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> kingdoms such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> and Mercia. London grew at its lowest bridging point, and its port made the city a world trading centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-thames.jpg" alt="River Thames" width="320" height="119" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diliff · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:London_Thames_Sunset_panorama_-_Feb_2008.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
